Build:     swift build -c release --force-resolved-versions \
               --scratch-path .build-worker --product mlxfast-runtime-worker
           git checkout -- Package.resolved

Knob (default OFF; unset => byte-identical behaviour and kernel names):

  NEZUKO_R93_PROBE=<target>:<kind>:<n>
    target  qkv | oproj | routed
    kind    fma | imad | ld8 | ld16
    n       0..64

  When set, the selected kernel's name gains the suffix `_pz<kind><n>` and a
  128 MiB uint32 probe pool is bound for EVERY kind, so the binding signature
  is identical across kinds and across n. Level n=0 is the name- and
  residency-matched placement control required by Rule 44: same name change,
  same extra binding, zero ladder rungs.

  fma / imad  add n*4 statements per K-loop iteration across 4 independent
              dependency chains, inside the kernel's main loop. Placed there
              deliberately: the scheduler may hide them in memory stalls, so
              the slope measures spare issue slots during those stalls.
  ld8 / ld16  add n strided uint2 / uint4 loads once per thread, after the
              epilogue, so the main loop's software pipelining is untouched
              and the slope is the marginal cost of a byte at the current
              operating point.

  The integer ladder is deliberately non-affine
  (`x = x*2654435761u + (x>>16)`); a repeated affine `x*C+D` would reassociate
  into a single mul-add and the ladder would collapse to one rung.

  Every kind terminates in a never-taken sink store
  `if (nz_sum > 3.0e38f) { sink[0] = bfloat(0.0f); }`, so the added work is
  bit-exact ADDITION (Rule 45): no existing work is deleted or weakened to
  price it. All census arms were checked for token divergence.

